// Laws of Motion (Physics) - Question Bank
// Format follows the same schema used across the practice app:
// { id, type, question, options, correctIndex, explanation, difficulty }
// type: 'mcq' | 'assertion' | 'matching' | 'statement' | 'count'
// Note: theory-focused, with only simple/light numerical questions included, per request.

export const LAWS_MOTION = [

  // ============ ARISTOTLE'S FALLACY AND THE LAW OF INERTIA ============
  { id: 'lom-1', type: 'mcq', question: 'Aristotle\'s view of motion held that a continuous force was necessary to keep a body moving; this idea is now known to be:', options: ['Correct, and forms the basis of modern mechanics', 'A fallacy, since a body in motion can continue moving without any external force if friction/resistance is absent', 'Only applicable to circular motion, not linear motion', 'Only applicable to objects in space, not on Earth'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Aristotle\'s belief that a continuous force is required to maintain motion is known as Aristotle\'s fallacy; in reality, an object in motion continues at constant velocity in the absence of a net external force.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-2', type: 'mcq', question: 'Galileo\'s studies of motion, using inclined planes, helped establish the concept that in the absence of friction (an idealised scenario), a moving object would continue to move:', options: ['Forever, with no change in its state of motion, unless acted upon by an external force', 'Only for a limited time, even without friction', 'Only in a circular path, never in a straight line', 'Only if continuously pushed'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Galileo\'s experiments led to the idea that, without friction, an object in motion would continue moving indefinitely, laying the groundwork for the concept of inertia.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-3', type: 'mcq', question: 'The tendency of an object to resist any change in its state of rest or uniform motion is called:', options: ['Inertia', 'Momentum', 'Impulse', 'Equilibrium'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Inertia refers to the inherent tendency of any object to resist a change in its state of rest or uniform motion.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-4', type: 'mcq', question: 'The mass of an object is considered a direct measure of its:', options: ['Inertia', 'Weight, exclusively', 'Momentum, exclusively', 'Velocity'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Mass is a quantitative measure of an object\'s inertia — the greater the mass, the greater its resistance to changes in its state of motion.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-5', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): A heavier object generally requires a greater force to produce the same acceleration as a lighter object.\nReason (R): Since mass is a measure of inertia, and inertia represents resistance to changes in motion, a greater mass implies a greater resistance to acceleration for a given applied force.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The relationship between mass and inertia correctly explains why heavier objects require greater force to achieve the same acceleration as lighter ones.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-6', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about inertia and Aristotle\'s fallacy:\n1. Aristotle believed that continuous force is required to sustain motion.\n2. Galileo\'s experiments contributed to the modern understanding of inertia.\n3. Inertia refers to an object\'s tendency to resist changes in its state of motion.\n4. Mass has no relationship with inertia.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct. Statement 4 is wrong — mass is a direct measure of inertia.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ NEWTON'S FIRST LAW OF MOTION ============
  { id: 'lom-7', type: 'mcq', question: 'Newton\'s First Law of Motion states that an object remains in a state of rest or uniform motion in a straight line unless it is acted upon by:', options: ['A net external force', 'Its own internal energy alone', 'Gravity exclusively, regardless of other forces', 'No force is ever required to change its state, according to this law'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Newton\'s First Law states that a body continues in its state of rest or uniform motion in a straight line unless compelled by a net external force to change that state.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-8', type: 'mcq', question: 'Newton\'s First Law of Motion is also commonly known as the:', options: ['Law of inertia', 'Law of acceleration, exclusively', 'Law of action and reaction', 'Law of conservation of energy'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Newton\'s First Law is often referred to as the law of inertia, since it defines and relies on the concept of inertia.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-9', type: 'mcq', question: 'According to Newton\'s First Law, in the absence of any net external force, a moving object will:', options: ['Continue moving with the same velocity (constant speed and direction)', 'Gradually slow down and eventually stop, even without any force acting', 'Immediately stop moving', 'Begin to accelerate spontaneously'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'In the absence of a net external force, an object in motion continues at constant velocity, neither speeding up, slowing down, nor changing direction.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-10', type: 'mcq', question: 'Newton\'s First Law is fundamentally important because it also provides a qualitative definition of:', options: ['Force, as that which changes (or tends to change) the state of rest or motion of a body', 'Energy, as a purely scalar quantity', 'Work, in terms of distance alone', 'Mass, without any reference to force'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Newton\'s First Law implicitly defines force as the external influence needed to change (or tend to change) a body\'s state of rest or uniform motion.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-11', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): Passengers standing in a bus tend to fall backward when the bus suddenly starts moving forward.\nReason (R): Due to inertia, the passenger\'s body tends to remain in its original state of rest, while the bus (and the lower part of the passenger\'s body in contact with it) suddenly moves forward, causing an apparent backward fall.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The tendency of the body to resist a sudden change in its state of rest (inertia) correctly explains why passengers appear to fall backward when a bus suddenly accelerates forward.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-12', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): Passengers in a moving bus tend to fall forward when the bus suddenly stops.\nReason (R): Due to inertia of motion, the passenger\'s upper body tends to continue moving forward even as the bus (and the lower part of the passenger in contact with it) suddenly comes to rest.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The inertia of motion, causing the body to continue moving forward despite the sudden stop of the bus, correctly explains the forward-falling tendency of passengers.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-13', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about Newton\'s First Law:\n1. It is also known as the law of inertia.\n2. It implies that a net external force is required to change an object\'s state of rest or uniform motion.\n3. It applies only to objects at rest, never to objects already in motion.\n4. It provides a qualitative definition of force.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 1, 2 and 4 are correct. Statement 3 is wrong — Newton\'s First Law applies equally to objects at rest and objects in uniform motion.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ MOMENTUM ============
  { id: 'lom-14', type: 'mcq', question: 'The physical quantity defined as the product of an object\'s mass and its velocity is called:', options: ['Momentum', 'Impulse', 'Force', 'Inertia'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Momentum (p) is defined as the product of mass (m) and velocity (v), i.e., p = mv.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-15', type: 'mcq', question: 'Momentum, like velocity, is classified as a:', options: ['Vector quantity', 'Scalar quantity', 'Dimensionless quantity', 'Fundamental (base) quantity'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Since momentum is the product of mass (a scalar) and velocity (a vector), it is itself a vector quantity, having the same direction as velocity.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-16', type: 'mcq', question: 'For a given velocity, an object with greater mass will have:', options: ['Greater momentum', 'Lesser momentum', 'The same momentum as any object, regardless of mass', 'Zero momentum, regardless of velocity'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Since momentum is directly proportional to mass (for a fixed velocity), a greater mass results in greater momentum.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-17', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): A fast-moving cricket ball can be more difficult to stop than a slow-moving truck, in certain specific circumstances.\nReason (R): Whether an object is easy or difficult to bring to rest depends specifically on its momentum, which is a product of both mass and velocity, not on mass or velocity alone.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The combined dependence of momentum on both mass and velocity correctly explains why comparisons of "difficulty to stop" cannot rely on mass or velocity in isolation.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-18', type: 'mcq', question: 'A ball of mass 2 kg moving with a velocity of 5 m/s has a momentum of:', options: ['10 kg m/s', '2.5 kg m/s', '7 kg m/s', '25 kg m/s'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Momentum = mass × velocity = 2 kg × 5 m/s = 10 kg m/s.', difficulty: 'easy' },

  // ============ NEWTON'S SECOND LAW OF MOTION ============
  { id: 'lom-19', type: 'mcq', question: 'Newton\'s Second Law of Motion states that the rate of change of momentum of an object is directly proportional to:', options: ['The net external force applied to it, and takes place in the direction of that force', 'The mass of the object alone, without reference to any applied force', 'The velocity of the object alone', 'The inertia of the object, without reference to force'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Newton\'s Second Law states that the rate of change of momentum of a body is proportional to the applied net force and occurs in the direction of that force.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-20', type: 'mcq', question: 'For a body of constant mass, Newton\'s Second Law can be simplified to the well-known equation:', options: ['F = ma (force equals mass times acceleration)', 'F = mv (force equals mass times velocity)', 'F = m/a', 'F = m + a'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'For constant mass, Newton\'s Second Law simplifies to F = ma, where F is net force, m is mass, and a is acceleration.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-21', type: 'mcq', question: 'According to Newton\'s Second Law, for a given mass, the acceleration produced in a body is:', options: ['Directly proportional to the net force applied', 'Inversely proportional to the net force applied', 'Completely independent of the net force applied', 'Always equal to zero, regardless of applied force'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'For a given mass, acceleration is directly proportional to the net applied force, as expressed in F = ma.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-22', type: 'mcq', question: 'According to Newton\'s Second Law, for a given net force, the acceleration produced in a body is:', options: ['Inversely proportional to the mass of the body', 'Directly proportional to the mass of the body', 'Completely independent of the mass of the body', 'Always equal to the mass of the body'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'For a fixed force, acceleration is inversely proportional to mass; heavier objects experience smaller acceleration for the same applied force.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-23', type: 'mcq', question: 'The SI unit of force, defined as the force required to produce an acceleration of 1 m/s² in a mass of 1 kg, is called the:', options: ['Newton', 'Dyne', 'Joule', 'Pascal'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The newton (N) is the SI unit of force, defined as the force needed to accelerate a 1 kg mass at 1 m/s².', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-24', type: 'mcq', question: 'Newton\'s First Law can be considered a special case of Newton\'s Second Law, corresponding to the situation where:', options: ['The net external force on a body is zero', 'The mass of the body is zero', 'The velocity of the body is at its maximum possible value', 'The body is undergoing circular motion exclusively'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'When the net external force is zero, Newton\'s Second Law (F = ma) implies zero acceleration, which corresponds exactly to Newton\'s First Law (constant velocity or rest).', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-25', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): Newton\'s Second Law is considered more general and fundamental than Newton\'s First Law.\nReason (R): Newton\'s First Law describes the special case of zero net force (zero acceleration), which is fully contained within and consistent with the more general force-acceleration relationship described by Newton\'s Second Law.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The fact that the First Law emerges as a special (zero-force) case of the Second Law correctly explains why the Second Law is considered more general.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-26', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): A larger force is required to bring a fast-moving heavy truck to rest within a given short time compared to a slow-moving light car.\nReason (R): According to Newton\'s Second Law, force is related to the rate of change of momentum, and a heavy, fast-moving truck has significantly greater momentum than a slow-moving light car, requiring a greater force to change that momentum within the same time interval.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The dependence of force on the rate of change of momentum, and the greater momentum of a heavy, fast truck, correctly explains why more force is needed to stop it in the same time.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-27', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about Newton\'s Second Law:\n1. For constant mass, F = ma.\n2. Acceleration is directly proportional to the net applied force, for a fixed mass.\n3. Acceleration is directly proportional to mass, for a fixed applied force.\n4. Newton\'s First Law can be seen as a special case of the Second Law, when net force is zero.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 1, 2 and 4 are correct. Statement 3 is wrong — acceleration is inversely proportional to mass for a fixed force, not directly proportional.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-28', type: 'mcq', question: 'A net force of 20 N is applied to an object of mass 4 kg. Using F = ma, the resulting acceleration is:', options: ['5 m/s²', '80 m/s²', '0.2 m/s²', '24 m/s²'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'a = F/m = 20 N / 4 kg = 5 m/s².', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-29', type: 'matching', question: 'Match Column I (Newtonian concept) with Column II (description):\nColumn I: A. First Law  B. Second Law  C. Momentum  D. Newton (unit)\nColumn II: i. Force needed for 1 m/s² acceleration of 1 kg mass  ii. Law of inertia  iii. F = ma  iv. Product of mass and velocity', options: ['A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i', 'A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv', 'A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii', 'A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'First Law — law of inertia; Second Law — F = ma; Momentum — product of mass and velocity; Newton — force unit definition.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ IMPULSE ============
  { id: 'lom-30', type: 'mcq', question: 'When a large force acts on an object for a very short interval of time, producing a finite change in momentum, the product of force and time is called:', options: ['Impulse', 'Work', 'Power', 'Torque'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Impulse is defined as the product of force and the (typically short) time interval over which it acts, and is equal to the resulting change in momentum.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-31', type: 'mcq', question: 'According to the impulse-momentum theorem, the impulse imparted to an object equals:', options: ['The change in the object\'s momentum', 'The object\'s final velocity alone, without reference to momentum', 'The object\'s mass alone', 'Zero, in all cases, regardless of force or time'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The impulse-momentum theorem states that impulse equals the change in momentum of the object, i.e., J = Δp.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-32', type: 'mcq', question: 'Impulse is a particularly useful concept for analysing situations involving:', options: ['Very large forces acting over very short durations, such as during a collision', 'Only constant, small forces acting over extremely long durations', 'Situations with absolutely no force acting at all', 'Only rotational motion, never linear motion'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Impulse is especially useful in analysing collisions and impacts, where large forces act over very brief time intervals.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-33', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): A cricketer draws their hands backward while catching a fast-moving ball.\nReason (R): By increasing the time over which the ball\'s momentum changes to zero, the cricketer reduces the average force experienced by their hands, since impulse (a fixed quantity for a given change in momentum) equals force multiplied by time.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The inverse relationship between force and time for a fixed impulse (change in momentum) correctly explains why increasing the time of contact reduces the force experienced.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-34', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about impulse:\n1. Impulse equals the product of force and the time interval over which it acts.\n2. Impulse equals the change in momentum of the object.\n3. Increasing the time of contact during a collision increases the average force experienced.\n4. Impulse is especially relevant in analysing short-duration events like collisions.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 1, 2 and 4 are correct. Statement 3 is wrong — for a fixed impulse, increasing the contact time decreases (not increases) the average force experienced.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ NEWTON'S THIRD LAW OF MOTION ============
  { id: 'lom-35', type: 'mcq', question: 'Newton\'s Third Law of Motion states that for every action, there is:', options: ['An equal and opposite reaction', 'A proportionally smaller reaction', 'No corresponding reaction at all', 'A reaction only if the objects are of equal mass'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Newton\'s Third Law states that for every action force, there is an equal and opposite reaction force.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-36', type: 'mcq', question: 'According to Newton\'s Third Law, the action and reaction forces between two interacting bodies:', options: ['Act on two different bodies (one on each body), and hence never cancel each other out', 'Always act on the same single body, and therefore cancel out', 'Only occur when both bodies are stationary', 'Are unrelated in magnitude, despite acting in opposite directions'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Action and reaction forces always act on two different bodies (one force on each), which is why they do not cancel each other, despite being equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-37', type: 'mcq', question: 'When a person pushes against a wall with a certain force, the wall exerts an equal and opposite force on the person; this scenario illustrates:', options: ['Newton\'s Third Law of Motion', 'Newton\'s First Law of Motion', 'The Law of Conservation of Energy', 'The Impulse-Momentum theorem, exclusively'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The mutual, equal-and-opposite forces between the person and the wall directly illustrate Newton\'s Third Law of Motion.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-38', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): Action and reaction forces, though equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, do not cancel each other and result in a net effect.\nReason (R): Since action and reaction forces act on two different bodies, each force independently affects the motion of the body it acts upon; they do not "cancel" because cancellation requires forces to act on the same body.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The fact that action and reaction forces act on different bodies correctly explains why they do not cancel out, despite being equal and opposite.', difficulty: 'hard' },
  { id: 'lom-39', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): A swimmer is able to propel themselves forward through water by pushing water backward.\nReason (R): By Newton\'s Third Law, as the swimmer exerts a backward force on the water, the water simultaneously exerts an equal and opposite (forward) reaction force on the swimmer, propelling them ahead.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The reaction force from the water, as described by Newton\'s Third Law, correctly explains how a swimmer moves forward while pushing water backward.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-40', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about Newton\'s Third Law:\n1. Action and reaction forces are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.\n2. Action and reaction forces act on the same object.\n3. Action and reaction forces occur simultaneously.\n4. Newton\'s Third Law applies only when both interacting objects are in motion.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Statements 1 and 3 are correct. Statement 2 is wrong (they act on different objects), and statement 4 is wrong (the law applies regardless of whether the objects are moving or at rest).', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ CONSERVATION OF MOMENTUM ============
  { id: 'lom-41', type: 'mcq', question: 'The Law of Conservation of Momentum states that in the absence of any external force, the total momentum of a system of interacting bodies:', options: ['Remains constant', 'Continuously increases', 'Continuously decreases', 'Becomes zero immediately'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'In the absence of an external force, the total momentum of a closed system remains constant, which is the Law of Conservation of Momentum.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-42', type: 'mcq', question: 'The Law of Conservation of Momentum can be derived directly as a consequence of combining Newton\'s:', options: ['Second and Third Laws of Motion', 'First Law alone, without reference to any other law', 'Only the concept of inertia, with no reference to Newton\'s laws', 'The definition of impulse alone, with no reference to any law'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The Law of Conservation of Momentum follows from Newton\'s Second Law (relating force to rate of change of momentum) combined with Newton\'s Third Law (equal and opposite forces between interacting bodies).', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-43', type: 'mcq', question: 'The recoil of a gun when a bullet is fired is a classic illustration of the:', options: ['Law of Conservation of Momentum', 'Law of Conservation of Energy, exclusively', 'Newton\'s First Law, exclusively', 'Concept of centripetal force'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The backward recoil of a gun, balancing the forward momentum of the fired bullet, is a direct illustration of the Law of Conservation of Momentum.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-44', type: 'mcq', question: 'In the recoil of a gun example, the total momentum of the bullet-gun system, before and immediately after firing, remains:', options: ['The same (conserved), since the initial total momentum was zero and no significant external force acts during the brief firing process', 'Different, since momentum is not conserved in this scenario', 'Undefined, since guns are outside the scope of momentum conservation', 'Doubled after firing, compared to before'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Since the initial momentum of the gun-bullet system is zero (both at rest) and negligible external horizontal force acts during firing, total momentum remains conserved, with the bullet\'s forward momentum balanced by the gun\'s backward momentum.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-45', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): The Law of Conservation of Momentum is a broadly applicable principle, extending beyond simple mechanical collisions.\nReason (R): Since the law derives from fundamental symmetry principles related to Newton\'s laws of motion, it applies to any isolated system of interacting bodies, regardless of the specific nature of the interaction (collision, explosion, or otherwise).', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The general applicability arising from underlying principles of Newtonian mechanics correctly explains the broad scope of momentum conservation, as stated in the assertion.', difficulty: 'hard' },
  { id: 'lom-46', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about conservation of momentum:\n1. Total momentum of an isolated system remains constant in the absence of external force.\n2. Conservation of momentum can be derived from Newton\'s Second and Third Laws.\n3. Recoil of a gun is an example illustrating conservation of momentum.\n4. Conservation of momentum applies only when the colliding bodies have equal mass.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct. Statement 4 is wrong — conservation of momentum applies regardless of whether the interacting bodies have equal or unequal mass.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ EQUILIBRIUM OF CONCURRENT FORCES ============
  { id: 'lom-47', type: 'mcq', question: 'A body is said to be in mechanical equilibrium when the vector sum of all forces acting on it is:', options: ['Zero', 'Maximum possible', 'Always equal to the object\'s weight', 'Undefined, unless the object is moving'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'A body is in mechanical equilibrium when the net (vector sum of) forces acting on it is zero.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-48', type: 'mcq', question: 'Forces that act on a body such that their lines of action pass through a common point are called:', options: ['Concurrent forces', 'Parallel forces exclusively', 'Non-concurrent forces exclusively', 'Impulsive forces exclusively'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Concurrent forces are forces whose lines of action intersect (pass through) a single common point.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-49', type: 'mcq', question: 'For a set of three concurrent, coplanar forces acting on a body in equilibrium, the vector representation of these three forces can be arranged to form a:', options: ['Closed triangle', 'Straight line only, in every case', 'Perfect circle', 'Open, incomplete polygon'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'For three concurrent forces in equilibrium, their vector representations, placed head to tail, form a closed triangle (a special case of the general condition for equilibrium of forces).', difficulty: 'hard' },
  { id: 'lom-50', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): A body can be in equilibrium even while multiple forces are acting on it simultaneously.\nReason (R): Equilibrium requires only that the net vector sum of all the individual forces acting on the body be zero, not that no forces act on the body at all.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The definition of equilibrium as zero net force (not zero individual forces) correctly explains how a body can be in equilibrium despite multiple forces acting on it.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ COMMON FORCES IN MECHANICS ============
  { id: 'lom-51', type: 'mcq', question: 'The force with which the Earth attracts an object towards its centre is called:', options: ['Gravitational force (weight)', 'Normal force', 'Tension', 'Frictional force'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Gravitational force is the attractive force exerted by the Earth on an object, commonly experienced as the object\'s weight.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-52', type: 'mcq', question: 'The contact force exerted by a surface on an object resting on it, acting perpendicular to the surface, is called:', options: ['Normal force', 'Tension', 'Frictional force', 'Gravitational force'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The normal force is the contact force exerted perpendicular to a surface, supporting an object resting on it.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-53', type: 'mcq', question: 'The force transmitted through a string, rope or cable when it is pulled taut, acting along its length, is called:', options: ['Tension', 'Normal force', 'Friction', 'Impulse'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Tension is the pulling force transmitted along a stretched string, rope or cable.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-54', type: 'mcq', question: 'The force that opposes the relative motion (or tendency of motion) between two surfaces in contact is called:', options: ['Friction', 'Normal force, exclusively', 'Tension, exclusively', 'Centripetal force'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Friction is the resistive force that opposes relative motion (or the tendency towards relative motion) between two surfaces in contact.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-55', type: 'mcq', question: 'The type of friction that acts between two surfaces in contact when there is no relative motion between them, but a tendency for one to slide over the other, is called:', options: ['Static friction', 'Kinetic (sliding) friction', 'Rolling friction', 'Fluid friction, exclusively'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Static friction acts when there is no actual relative motion between surfaces, but a force is applied that tends to cause sliding.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-56', type: 'mcq', question: 'The type of friction that acts between two surfaces already in relative motion (sliding) with respect to each other is called:', options: ['Static friction', 'Kinetic (sliding) friction', 'Rolling friction, exclusively', 'None; friction does not act during actual sliding'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Kinetic (sliding) friction acts between surfaces that are already moving relative to each other.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-57', type: 'mcq', question: 'The maximum value of static friction that can act just before an object begins to slide, is generally referred to as:', options: ['Limiting friction', 'Rolling friction', 'Fluid friction', 'Kinetic friction, incorrectly used as a synonym here'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Limiting friction is the maximum possible value of static friction, occurring just as an object is about to start sliding.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-58', type: 'mcq', question: 'For a given pair of surfaces, the maximum static friction (limiting friction) is generally observed to be:', options: ['Somewhat greater than the kinetic friction between the same surfaces', 'Always exactly equal to kinetic friction, with no difference', 'Always less than kinetic friction', 'Completely unrelated to kinetic friction'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'For most surfaces, the maximum static (limiting) friction is somewhat greater than the kinetic friction, meaning it generally takes more force to start motion than to sustain it.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-59', type: 'mcq', question: 'The type of friction that occurs when an object rolls (rather than slides) over a surface, generally much smaller in magnitude than sliding friction, is called:', options: ['Rolling friction', 'Static friction, exclusively', 'Kinetic (sliding) friction, exclusively', 'Fluid friction, exclusively'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Rolling friction occurs when an object rolls over a surface, and is generally much smaller than sliding (kinetic) friction between the same surfaces.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-60', type: 'mcq', question: 'The magnitude of kinetic friction acting between two surfaces is generally proportional to the:', options: ['Normal force (reaction force) between the two surfaces', 'Only the velocity of the moving object, and unrelated to normal force', 'Only the surface area of contact, and unrelated to normal force', 'The colour of the surfaces involved'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Kinetic friction is generally proportional to the normal force between the two surfaces, expressed as f = μk N, where μk is the coefficient of kinetic friction.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-61', type: 'mcq', question: 'The dimensionless constant of proportionality relating the friction force to the normal force between two surfaces is called the:', options: ['Coefficient of friction', 'Coefficient of restitution', 'Impulse constant', 'Tension coefficient'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The coefficient of friction (μ) is a dimensionless quantity relating the frictional force to the normal reaction force between two surfaces.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-62', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): It generally takes more force to start pushing a heavy box across the floor than to keep it moving once it has started sliding.\nReason (R): Since the maximum static (limiting) friction is generally greater than kinetic friction for the same pair of surfaces, more force is required to overcome static friction and initiate motion than to maintain motion against kinetic friction.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The generally higher value of limiting static friction compared to kinetic friction correctly explains why initiating motion requires more force than sustaining it.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-63', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): Rolling friction is generally much smaller than sliding friction for the same pair of surfaces.\nReason (R): In rolling motion, the point of contact between the rolling object and the surface has little to no relative sliding motion, reducing the resistive force compared to sliding friction, where surfaces slide past each other.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The reduced relative sliding at the contact point in rolling motion correctly explains why rolling friction is generally much smaller than sliding friction.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-64', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about friction:\n1. Static friction acts when there is no relative sliding motion between surfaces.\n2. Kinetic friction generally exceeds the maximum static (limiting) friction for the same surfaces.\n3. Rolling friction is generally smaller in magnitude than sliding friction.\n4. The coefficient of friction relates the frictional force to the normal force between surfaces.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 1, 3 and 4 are correct. Statement 2 is wrong — it is generally the limiting static friction that exceeds kinetic friction, not the other way around.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-65', type: 'matching', question: 'Match Column I (force type) with Column II (description):\nColumn I: A. Normal force  B. Tension  C. Static friction  D. Kinetic friction\nColumn II: i. Acts along a taut string/rope  ii. Acts between sliding surfaces  iii. Perpendicular contact force  iv. Acts when no relative sliding occurs, but tendency to slide exists', options: ['A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii', 'A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv', 'A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i', 'A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Normal force — perpendicular contact force; Tension — acts along taut string; Static friction — no relative sliding, tendency exists; Kinetic friction — acts between sliding surfaces.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ CIRCULAR MOTION DYNAMICS ============
  { id: 'lom-66', type: 'mcq', question: 'The net force directed towards the centre of a circular path, necessary to keep an object moving in that circular path, is called the:', options: ['Centripetal force', 'Centrifugal force', 'Tangential force, exclusively', 'Normal force, exclusively'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Centripetal force is the net force directed towards the centre of a circular path, required to keep an object moving along that circular trajectory.', difficulty: 'easy' },
  { id: 'lom-67', type: 'mcq', question: 'For a car moving along a flat, horizontal circular road, the necessary centripetal force is generally provided by:', options: ['Friction between the tyres and the road surface', 'The weight of the car alone, with no contribution from friction', 'Only the normal force from the road, with no contribution from friction', 'Air resistance acting on the car'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'On a flat circular road, friction between the tyres and road surface provides the centripetal force needed for the car to move along the curved path.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-68', type: 'mcq', question: 'Roads at curves are often "banked" (built at an angle, higher on the outer edge), primarily to:', options: ['Provide an additional (or alternative) source of centripetal force, reducing dependence on friction', 'Make the road more visually appealing, with no functional benefit', 'Increase the friction between tyres and the road unnecessarily', 'Slow down vehicles at every curve, regardless of speed'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Banking of roads provides a component of the normal force directed towards the centre of the curve, supplementing (or reducing dependence on) friction as a source of centripetal force.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-69', type: 'mcq', question: 'For a banked road (with friction present), there exists an optimum speed at which a vehicle can safely navigate the curve, relying:', options: ['Solely on the horizontal component of the normal force for centripetal force, without needing friction', 'Solely on friction, with the banking angle playing no role', 'Solely on gravity, with no contribution from the normal force', 'On neither friction nor the normal force, since no force is needed at the optimum speed'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'At the optimum (design) speed for a banked road, the horizontal component of the normal force alone provides the necessary centripetal force, without requiring any friction.', difficulty: 'hard' },
  { id: 'lom-70', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): Banking of roads at curves helps vehicles negotiate turns more safely, especially at higher speeds.\nReason (R): The banking angle provides a component of the normal force directed towards the centre of the curved path, supplementing the centripetal force and reducing the reliance on friction, which can be unreliable in wet or slippery conditions.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The role of banking in supplementing centripetal force and reducing friction dependence correctly explains its safety benefit for vehicles navigating curves, especially at speed.', difficulty: 'hard' },
  { id: 'lom-71', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about circular motion dynamics:\n1. Centripetal force is directed towards the centre of the circular path.\n2. On a flat road, friction provides the necessary centripetal force for a turning vehicle.\n3. Banking of roads eliminates the need for centripetal force entirely.\n4. At the optimum speed on a banked, frictionless road, the horizontal component of the normal force alone provides centripetal force.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 1, 2 and 4 are correct. Statement 3 is wrong — banking does not eliminate the need for centripetal force; it provides an alternative/additional source for it.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ APPARENT WEIGHT AND MOTION IN A LIFT ============
  { id: 'lom-72', type: 'mcq', question: 'The reading shown by a weighing scale (spring balance) that a person stands on, which may differ from their true (gravitational) weight under certain conditions, is called the:', options: ['Apparent weight', 'True weight, exclusively, with no distinction from apparent weight', 'Centripetal weight', 'Normal weight, used interchangeably with true weight always'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Apparent weight refers to the weight registered by a scale, which can differ from an object\'s true weight when there is relative acceleration, such as in an accelerating lift.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-73', type: 'mcq', question: 'When a lift (elevator) accelerates upward, the apparent weight of a person standing inside it, as measured by a weighing scale, is:', options: ['Greater than their true weight', 'Less than their true weight', 'Exactly equal to their true weight, regardless of the lift\'s acceleration', 'Always zero, regardless of the lift\'s motion'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'During upward acceleration of a lift, the normal force (and hence apparent weight) exceeds the true gravitational weight of the person, since additional upward force is needed for the upward acceleration.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-74', type: 'mcq', question: 'When a lift (elevator) accelerates downward, the apparent weight of a person standing inside it, as measured by a weighing scale, is:', options: ['Less than their true weight', 'Greater than their true weight', 'Exactly equal to their true weight, in every case of downward acceleration', 'Impossible to determine, without knowing the lift\'s exact speed'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'During downward acceleration, the normal force (and hence apparent weight) is less than the true weight, since less upward force is needed to produce the downward acceleration.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-75', type: 'mcq', question: 'If a lift were to fall freely under gravity alone (a state of free fall), the apparent weight of a person inside it would become:', options: ['Zero, resulting in a sensation of weightlessness', 'Equal to twice their true weight', 'Equal to their true weight, unchanged', 'Undefined, since apparent weight has no meaning during free fall'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'During free fall, the normal force (and hence apparent weight) becomes zero, since the person and the lift accelerate downward together at exactly g, producing a sensation of weightlessness.', difficulty: 'hard' },
  { id: 'lom-76', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): A person\'s apparent weight can differ from their true weight without any actual change in their mass.\nReason (R): Apparent weight is determined by the normal force exerted by the supporting surface (such as a lift floor), which depends on the acceleration of the system, whereas true weight (mg) depends only on mass and gravitational acceleration.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The distinction between the (acceleration-dependent) normal force and the fixed gravitational weight correctly explains why apparent weight can vary without any actual change in mass.', difficulty: 'hard' },
  { id: 'lom-77', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements about apparent weight in a lift:\n1. Apparent weight increases when the lift accelerates upward.\n2. Apparent weight decreases when the lift accelerates downward.\n3. Apparent weight becomes zero during free fall of the lift.\n4. Apparent weight always equals true weight, regardless of the lift\'s acceleration.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct. Statement 4 is wrong — apparent weight can differ from true weight when the lift accelerates.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-78', type: 'statement', question: 'Statement I: A person feels heavier than usual when a lift starts moving upward with acceleration.\nStatement II: This sensation arises because the normal force from the lift floor on the person is greater than the person\'s true weight during upward acceleration.\nIn light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer:', options: ['Both Statement I and Statement II are correct, and Statement II correctly explains the sensation described in Statement I', 'Both statements are correct but unrelated', 'Statement I is correct, Statement II is incorrect', 'Statement I is incorrect, Statement II is correct'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The increased normal force during upward lift acceleration (Statement II) directly and correctly explains the sensation of increased apparent weight described in Statement I.', difficulty: 'medium' },

  // ============ MIXED / REVISION ============
  { id: 'lom-79', type: 'mcq', question: 'Which of the following correctly matches each of Newton\'s three laws with its core concept?', options: ['First Law — inertia; Second Law — F=ma; Third Law — action-reaction pairs', 'First Law — F=ma; Second Law — inertia; Third Law — momentum conservation', 'First Law — action-reaction; Second Law — inertia; Third Law — F=ma', 'All three laws describe exactly the same concept, with no distinction'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'This correctly summarises the core concept of each of Newton\'s three laws: inertia (First), F=ma (Second), and action-reaction pairs (Third).', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-80', type: 'mcq', question: 'Which of the following pairs is INCORRECTLY matched?', options: ['Impulse — Change in momentum', 'Centripetal force — Directed away from the centre of a circular path', 'Static friction — Acts before relative sliding begins', 'Newton — SI unit of force'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'Centripetal force is directed towards, not away from, the centre of the circular path; a force directed away from the centre would not keep an object in circular motion.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-81', type: 'assertion', question: 'Assertion (A): The three laws of motion, together, form the foundation of classical (Newtonian) mechanics.\nReason (R): The First Law defines the concept of a net force through inertia, the Second Law quantitatively relates force to the resulting change in motion, and the Third Law describes the mutual nature of forces between interacting bodies — together providing a complete framework for analysing motion.', options: ['Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A', 'A is true but R is false', 'A is false but R is true'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'The complementary roles of the three laws, together forming a complete framework for analysing motion, correctly explain why they collectively form the foundation of classical mechanics.', difficulty: 'medium' },
  { id: 'lom-82', type: 'count', question: 'Consider the following statements summarising the chapter:\n1. Newton\'s laws of motion apply universally to all reference frames, including non-inertial (accelerating) ones, without modification.\n2. The concept of force is intimately connected with the concept of momentum and its rate of change.\n3. Friction, tension, and normal force are all examples of contact forces.\n4. Conservation of momentum is a consequence of Newton\'s Second and Third Laws.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?', options: ['One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct. Statement 1 is wrong — Newton\'s laws, in their standard form, apply directly to inertial frames; non-inertial (accelerating) frames require the introduction of pseudo-forces for the laws to hold in the same form.', difficulty: 'hard' },
  { id: 'lom-83', type: 'statement', question: 'Statement I: In a non-inertial (accelerating) frame of reference, Newton\'s laws of motion do not hold in their standard form unless a pseudo-force is introduced.\nStatement II: A pseudo-force is a fictitious force introduced to account for the effects of the frame\'s own acceleration, and does not arise from any real physical interaction between bodies.\nIn light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer:', options: ['Both Statement I and Statement II are correct', 'Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect', 'Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect', 'Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'], correctIndex: 0, explanation: 'Both statements correctly describe the concept and purpose of pseudo-forces in non-inertial reference frames.', difficulty: 'hard' },
];
